    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hi, i'm looking for some clarification on how inheritance tax works for assets held in different jurisdictions. specifically, if a deceased individual was a UK resident but held significant real estate in Spain and liquid assets in a US-based brokerage account, how is the global estate typically treated for UK IHT purposes?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Inheritance tax (IHT) considerations for a resident of the UK with global assets can be complex, especially when involving multiple jurisdictions like Spain and the US. For your UK IHT liabilities, the value of your worldwide assets may be included in the estate's assessment, but Spain will apply its own Inheritance Tax. In Spain, assets located within its jurisdiction are taxed according to Spanish law regardless of your residence. In contrast, the US may also levy its own estate tax or inheritance tax on worldwide assets owned by individuals domiciled outside of the US. The UK's HMRC will assess IHT on UK-domiciled assets not specifically built-in heir exemptions, potentially leading to multiple layered tax considerations, so it's essential to consult an international tax advisor.",
      "source": "target",
