    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. i'm a bit worried about the \"layered\" part. are there any specific scenarios where an asset might be overlooked or where tax liabilities could come as a total surprise? like, are there any common ways people end up with unexpected tax bills in these kinds of international setups?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In international estate situations, the tax landscape can indeed be complex, leading to potential oversights or unexpected liabilities. One common scenario arises from the failure to recognize the difference between domicile and residence for IHT purposes; individuals may underestimate their global estate because they consider only their UK residence. Another scenario involves misinterpreting the EU Succession Regulation which came into effect on May 17, 2019. Although Spain has not yet aligned with the EU Regulation, for other EU-based assets, it could simplify probate matters and estate settlements. On the double taxation front, while the UK has an Estate Duty (also called IHT) and the US has an Estate Tax (and sometimes a separate series of state inheritance taxes), these taxes typically have individual residence rules. For example, properties in the the Caribbean  being subject to \"Death Tax\" such as Bermuda can result in an overlooked inheritance tax by residents not frequently traveling across their borders.\n\n\nMoreover, trusts, life insurance policies, and gifts—especially those made up to seven years before death—could have tax implications when foreign banks unwittingly report to the IRS and not the UK, resulting in a tax burden that was initially overlooked. Complexity intensifies with digital assets, which can sometimes be inherited by default",
      "source": "target",
